course: technology
lesson: 7
title: Toys and Lego
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A Lego brick begins as a handful of plastic grains, melted and squirted at huge pressure into a steel mould, cooled in seconds and dropped out finished: this is called injection moulding.
explain: The moulds are so exact that a brick made in 1958 still clicks onto one made today, which is why your grandad's old bricks fit your new set.
explain: Most plastic toys, from action figures to board game pieces, are made the same way, and one expensive steel mould can make millions of copies.
explain: Before a toy reaches a shop, testers pull, drop, chew-test and even burn samples on purpose, and a mark like CE or UKCA shows the maker says it meets the safety rules.
explain: Small bricks, caps and burst balloon pieces are a real choking danger for babies and toddlers, so toys for little ones have no small parts and eyes that cannot be pulled off.
fact: Injection moulding pushes hot melted plastic into a closed metal mould, cools it and pushes the part out; the same mould is used millions of times.
fact: A good steel mould can cost more than a car, because it must be shaped extremely precisely and last for millions of parts.
fact: Lego bricks are made so precisely that a brick from 1958 still fits a brick made today; Lego has factories in Denmark, Hungary, the Czech Republic, Mexico, China and Vietnam.
fact: Small bricks, caps and burst balloon pieces are a choking danger for babies and toddlers, so they must be kept away from little ones.
fact: Teddy bears are sewn inside out from machine-cut pieces, turned, stuffed with fibre blown in by a machine and stitched shut; toys for little ones have eyes locked on from inside so they cannot be pulled off.
fact: The teddy bear is named after US President Theodore "Teddy" Roosevelt.
fact: A CE or UKCA mark shows the maker says the toy meets safety rules on small parts, sharp edges, chemicals and fire; testers pull, drop, chew-test and burn sample toys on purpose, and fake toys may skip the tests.
fact: Dolls and action figures are moulded in parts, painted by machines and by hand, sometimes with tiny stamping pads one colour at a time, and clicked together.
fact: A remote-control car's controller sends radio signals that a receiver in the car turns into commands for small motors; different cars use different channels so they do not control each other.
fact: Rechargeable toy batteries should be charged on a hard surface with an adult around.
fact: Shop slime is a gooey mix of glue-like chemicals and water that holds a shape for a moment but flows slowly, neither properly solid nor liquid; never eat it, and wash your hands after.
fact: Board game boards are printed on paper glued to cardboard, and cards are printed on big sheets and cut out; Monopoly has been printed in dozens of languages.
fact: Balloons are made by dipping balloon-shaped formers (solid shapes to coat) in liquid latex, the sap of rubber trees; they shrink over a few days because the gas slowly escapes through the rubber.
fact: A 3D printer melts plastic string and lays it down layer by thin layer following a computer drawing; its nozzle gets very hot, so adults handle it.
exercise: How is a Lego brick made? | Melted plastic is squirted into a steel mould and cooled | It is carved out of a solid plastic block by a fast robot arm | It is printed layer by layer in a 3D printer | It is baked from clay and then painted
exercise: What is injection moulding? | Pushing hot melted plastic into a closed mould to shape it | Blowing up a soft hot plastic tube inside a mould like a balloon | Sewing plastic sheets together with thread | Dipping a shape into liquid rubber
exercise: Why are toy moulds so expensive? | They must be shaped very precisely in steel and last millions of uses | They are made of solid gold | Each mould can be used only once | They are carved from diamonds by hand
exercise: Will a Lego brick from 1958 fit a brick made today? | Yes, the sizes have stayed exact | No, the studs changed size in 2000 | Only if you glue them together | Only if both bricks are red
exercise: Which list names countries with Lego factories? | Denmark, Hungary, Mexico and China | Brazil, Egypt, India and Peru | Japan, Canada, Norway and Spain | Kenya, Chile, Iran and Greece
exercise: Why must small bricks be kept away from babies? | Babies can choke on them | Bricks give off a poison gas | Bricks stay hot from the factory | Bricks carry electricity
exercise: How are teddy bear eyes fixed on toys for little children? | Locked on from inside so they cannot be pulled off | Sewn on with one loose thread so they can be swapped | Stuck on with ordinary paper glue from a school pot | Pinned on with sewing pins pushed in from the front
exercise: Who is the teddy bear named after? | US President Theodore "Teddy" Roosevelt | A zookeeper called Teddy Smith | The first bear at London Zoo | A toy maker called Ted Bear
exercise: What does a CE or UKCA mark on a toy show? | The maker says it meets the safety rules | The toy was made only in England and nowhere else | The toy is meant only for adults, not for children | The toy has won a prize for its clever design
exercise: How do toy testers check toys? | They pull, drop, chew-test and burn samples on purpose | They ask the factory whether it is safe | They only read the writing on the box and check the price | They give toys to toddlers and wait for accidents
exercise: How are tiny faces painted on action figures? | By machines and by hand, sometimes with tiny stamping pads | By shop staff after the figures are sold | By bright sunlight in the factory yard, which colours the plastic | By the buyer, with felt-tip pens
exercise: How does a remote-control car get its commands? | Radio signals from the controller reach a receiver in the car | An invisible string pulls it along | It listens to your voice through a tiny microphone in its roof | Magnets under the floor steer it
exercise: Why doesn't your remote drive your friend's car? | Different cars use different radio channels | Each car obeys only the voice of the child who owns it | Every remote has a tiny lock and its own matching key | The batteries inside the two cars are a different colour
exercise: Why is slime called neither solid nor liquid? | It holds a shape for a moment but flows slowly like a liquid | It is half ice and half water, held together with sticky glue | It is a gas trapped in jelly | It changes when you say its name
exercise: How are game cards made? | Printed on big sheets and cut out | Each card is drawn by hand | They are moulded like Lego bricks | They are cut from thin wooden boards
exercise: How are balloons made? | Formers are dipped in liquid latex from rubber trees | They are blown from melted glass | They are cut out from old plastic carrier bags and sealed | They are knitted from rubber thread
exercise: Why does a balloon get smaller after a few days? | The gas slowly escapes through the rubber | The air inside gets tired and shrinks down | The rubber slowly grows thicker and squeezes it | The air turns into drops of water inside the balloon
exercise: How does a 3D printer build a toy? | It lays melted plastic string layer by layer from a drawing | It carves the toy out of a solid block, just like a sculptor | It pours plastic into a steel mould | It sews plastic threads together
test: «baby sis wants my Lego, she's 1, she'll be fine» What is right? | No, small bricks are a choking danger; keep them away from her | Fine, babies cannot swallow bricks | Fine, as long as they are the big red ones that don't fit in mouths | Fine, as long as she is sitting down
test: «got this mega cheap robot toy online, no marks on it, whatever» What is the safer thought? | A toy without proper safety marks may have skipped the tests | Every toy is safe wherever it comes from | Cheaper toys are always the safest | Safety marks are just stickers for decoration, they don't mean anything
test: «balloon popped, me little bro's chewing a bit, aww» What should you do? | Take it away and tell an adult; balloon bits can choke little ones | Leave him to it, balloon rubber is soft and perfectly safe to chew on | Give him a whole new balloon instead | Let him try to blow the bit up again
test: «wanna touch the 3D printer nozzle, see the plastic come out» What is the rule? | Never: the nozzle gets very hot, so adults handle it | Fine, the plastic always comes out of the nozzle cold | Fine, as long as you only touch it with the tip of one finger | Only touch it while it is moving, as it cools down then
test: Grandad found his Lego from the 1970s. Will it click onto your new set? | Yes, the sizes have stayed the same since 1958 | No, old bricks are a different size | Only if you warm them first | Only with other old bricks
test: What would happen if a toy factory could use each steel mould only once? | Every toy would cost a fortune, because moulds are very expensive | Toys would get cheaper | Toys would get much stronger | Nothing would change at all
test: «slime's like jelly, bet it tastes nice» What is right? | Never eat it, and wash your hands after playing | It is fine to eat a little bit | It is made of melted jelly sweets, so it is like pudding | Only green slime is bad to eat
test: Each remote-control car has 4 wheels. How many wheels are on 6 cars? | 24 | 10 | 20 | 46
test: A 2-by-4 Lego brick has 8 studs on top. How many studs are on 3 such bricks? | 24 | 11 | 12 | 32
test: In a film a toddler pulls the button eyes off a teddy. Why don't real baby toys allow this? | Their eyes are locked on from inside, so they can't come off and be swallowed | Button eyes are too expensive | Babies are scared of eyes | Real teddies have no eyes at all
test: «my mate's RC car keeps driving off when I use my remote» What is the likely reason? | Both cars are on the same radio channel | His car is haunted and drives off on its own | My remote is far too strong for his little car | Our batteries swapped over when we weren't looking
test: «toy charger's on me bed under the duvet, it'll be fine» What is the safe rule? | Charge rechargeable toy batteries on a hard surface with an adult around | Under a duvet is best, because it keeps the battery nice and warm while charging | Charging is fine anywhere at all | Charge it in the bath so it stays cool
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises injection moulding? | Molten plastic is forced under pressure into a closed metal mould, cooled, and ejected | Plastic sheets are stitched together over a frame | Liquid rubber is coated upon a former by dipping | Plastic string is deposited in successive layers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a balloon diminishes in size over several days. | The gas within escapes gradually through the rubber | The rubber absorbs moisture from the air and thickens accordingly | The air within is converted into water | The balloon is consumed by bacteria
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a CE or UKCA mark upon a toy. | It signifies the manufacturer's declaration that the toy satisfies safety requirements | It signifies that the toy was manufactured exclusively within the United Kingdom by British firms | It signifies that the toy is intended solely for adults | It signifies that the toy has been awarded a prize
exam: Determine the aggregate number of wheels upon seven toy cars, it being given that each car possesses four wheels. | Twenty-eight | Eleven | Twenty-four | Thirty-two
